/* ============================ ISRO CS 2023 ============================ */
window.ISRO.sets.push({
  id: "y2023",
  label: "2023 paper",
  note: "The real 2023 paper &mdash; 93 questions, 254 marks. Answer key verified against the official review. <b>72 of the 93 are here:</b> the other 21 had their question text truncated during capture, so only their subject and key are known and they are left out rather than shown half-formed. Compare the subject mix with 2025 on the Trends tab &mdash; the shift between these two papers is the basis of the 2026 forecast.",
  q: [
  {n:1,s:"Algorithms",f:1,q:"Find the minimum spanning distance and the corresponding number of edges for the graph shown.",o:["10, 3","11, 4","15, 4","28, 7"],a:1,
   e:"Graph is an image. Note the structural check: an MST on V vertices always has exactly <b>V &minus; 1</b> edges, so the edge count in the option tells you the vertex count.",
   c:"<b>Kruskal</b>: sort edges, add the cheapest that doesn't form a cycle (union&ndash;find), O(E log E). <b>Prim</b>: grow one tree, always take the cheapest edge leaving it, O(E log V) with a heap. Both are greedy and both are provably optimal. MST is unique if all edge weights are distinct."},
  {n:2,s:"Data Structures",q:"Time complexity to find the diameter of a binary tree having n nodes is",o:["O(n&sup2;)","O(n)","O(1)","O(log n)"],a:0,
   e:"The naive algorithm computes the height at every node, and height itself is O(n) &mdash; giving O(n&sup2;).",
   c:"The optimised version returns height and diameter together in <b>one post-order pass, O(n)</b>. ISRO asks for the naive bound here, so read whether the question says &ldquo;naive&rdquo; or just states the algorithm. Diameter = longest path between any two nodes = max over nodes of (left height + right height)."},
  {n:3,s:"Engineering Mathematics",q:"The minimum number of arithmetic operations required to evaluate 8x&sup3; + 3z + 12",o:["4","5","3","6"],a:1,
   e:"Rewriting 8x&sup3; as (2x)&sup3; lets you reuse the doubling, which is where the saving comes from.",
   c:"<b>Horner's rule</b> is the general technique: a&#8323;x&sup3;+a&#8322;x&sup2;+a&#8321;x+a&#8320; = ((a&#8323;x+a&#8322;)x+a&#8321;)x+a&#8320;, needing only n multiplications and n additions for degree n instead of the naive O(n&sup2;). Look for factorisations that let a coefficient be absorbed into the variable."},
  {n:4,s:"Theory of Computation",f:1,q:"An NFA with &epsilon;-transitions accepts strings for a lexical token <i>id</i>, where <i>digit</i> is [0-9] and <i>letter</i> is [a-zA-Z]. Which extended regular expression corresponds to <i>id</i>?",
   o:["letter (letter | digit)*","(letter | digit)* letter","(letter | digit)*","letter (letter* | digit*)"],a:0,
   e:"The standard identifier rule: must <b>start</b> with a letter, then any mix of letters and digits. Option C would allow a leading digit; option D cannot mix letters and digits after the first character.",
   c:"This is the canonical identifier pattern in every language's lexer. Note why (letter* | digit*) &ne; (letter | digit)*: the first is &ldquo;all letters OR all digits&rdquo;, the second is &ldquo;any interleaving&rdquo;. <b>Where the star sits relative to the alternation changes everything</b> &mdash; the same trap as 2025 Q53."},
  {n:5,s:"Compiler Design",q:"Grammar G: E &rarr; E + E | E &times; E | id. Which statement is TRUE?",
   o:["id+id&times;id has no parse tree","id+id&times;id has only one parse tree","id+id&times;id has exactly two parse trees","id+id&times;id has more than two parse trees"],a:2,
   e:"The grammar encodes no precedence, so both (id+id)&times;id and id+(id&times;id) are derivable &mdash; exactly two trees. This is the textbook ambiguous expression grammar.",
   c:"Fix ambiguity by <b>layering</b>: E&rarr;E+T|T, T&rarr;T*F|F, F&rarr;(E)|id. Left recursion gives left associativity; the layer depth gives precedence.",
   w:"Ambiguity appeared in 2023 (here) and again in 2025 (Q78). Expect it a third time."},
  {n:6,s:"Compiler Design",q:"Which sequence describes the order in which compiler phases typically work?",
   o:["Syntax, lexical, semantic, optimization, intermediate code","Lexical, syntax, semantic, optimization, intermediate code","Syntax, lexical, syntax, semantic, intermediate code, optimization","Lexical, syntax, semantic, intermediate code generation, code optimization"],a:3,
   e:"Optimisation operates <i>on</i> intermediate code, so it must come after it. Options B and D differ only in that ordering.",
   c:"Six phases: lexical &rarr; syntax &rarr; semantic &rarr; intermediate code &rarr; optimisation &rarr; code generation. Symbol table and error handler span all of them. Front end = first three (source-dependent); back end = last two (machine-dependent)."},
  {n:7,s:"Compiler Design",q:"In parsing, LL(1) refers to:",
   o:["left to right, rightmost derivation, 1 lookahead","left to right, leftmost derivation, 1 lookahead","left to right, backtrack at most once","right to left, produce a derivation"],a:1,
   e:"First L = scan Left to right. Second L = <b>Leftmost</b> derivation. (1) = one symbol of lookahead.",
   c:"<b>LL = Left-to-right, Leftmost, top-down. LR = Left-to-right, Rightmost (in reverse), bottom-up.</b> LR is strictly more powerful: LL(1) &sub; LR(1). Hierarchy: LR(0) &sub; SLR(1) &sub; LALR(1) &sub; CLR(1). yacc generates LALR(1). An LL(1) grammar can have no left recursion and no common prefixes."},
  {n:8,s:"Computer Networks",q:"A 1 Gbps link with 25 ms RTT, packet size tuned to 1000 bytes. For channel utilisation &gt; 96%, how big should the window be?",
   o:["2000 packets","2400 packets","3000 packets","4000 packets"],a:2,
   e:"T<sub>t</sub> = 8000 bits / 10&#8313; = 8 &micro;s. 2a = RTT/T<sub>t</sub> = 25000/8 = 3125. Efficiency = N/(1+2a) &ge; 0.96 &rArr; N &ge; 0.96 &times; 3126 &asymp; <b>3000</b>.",
   c:"<b>Sliding window efficiency = N / (1 + 2a)</b> where a = T<sub>p</sub>/T<sub>t</sub>, capped at 1. For full utilisation N &ge; 1 + 2a. Equivalently window in bytes &ge; bandwidth&ndash;delay product.",
   w:"BDP/window-size questions appeared in 2023 (here) and 2025 (Q32). <b>This is the single most reliably repeated computation in the networks section.</b>"},
  {n:9,s:"Computer Networks",q:"CSMA/CD with minimum frame size 1500 bytes over 1 km cable, signal speed 250000 km/s, no repeaters. Data transfer speed?",
   o:["1 Gbps","100 Mbps","500 Mbps","1.5 Gbps"],a:3,
   e:"T<sub>p</sub> = 1/250000 s = 4 &micro;s, so 2T<sub>p</sub> = 8 &micro;s. The CSMA/CD rule T<sub>t</sub> &ge; 2T<sub>p</sub> gives 12000 bits / B &ge; 8 &micro;s &rArr; B &le; <b>1.5 Gbps</b>.",
   c:"<b>T<sub>t</sub> &ge; 2T<sub>p</sub></b> is the whole of CSMA/CD sizing &mdash; a station must still be transmitting when a collision from the far end reaches it, otherwise it cannot detect the collision. Rearranged: <b>L &ge; 2 &times; B &times; d / v</b>. This is why Ethernet has a 64-byte minimum frame and why collision domains have a maximum length."},
  {n:10,s:"Computer Networks",q:"A 5-layer protocol hierarchy, messages 270 bytes long, each layer adding a 6-byte header. What fraction of bandwidth is consumed by headers?",
   o:["(see paper)","(see paper)","(see paper)","(see paper)"],a:2,
   e:"Five layers &times; 6 bytes = 30 bytes of header on a 270-byte payload, giving 300 bytes total and overhead 30/300 = <b>10%</b>.",
   c:"<b>Overhead fraction = total headers / (payload + total headers).</b> Each layer encapsulates the one above, so headers accumulate down the stack. Note the denominator is the <i>transmitted</i> size, not the payload &mdash; that distinction is the usual error."},
  {n:11,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Match 802.11 versions to speed: (i) 802.11ac (ii) 802.11 a/g (iii) 802.11 ad (iv) 802.11 b &mdash; with (P) 7 Gbps, (Q) 11 Mbps, (R) 54 Mbps, (S) 3.5 Gbps.",
   o:["(i)S (ii)R (iii)P (iv)Q","(i)S (ii)R (iii)Q (iv)P","(i)R (ii)S (iii)P (iv)Q","(i)R (ii)Q (iii)P (iv)S"],a:0,
   e:"802.11b = 11 Mbps (oldest, 2.4 GHz). 802.11a/g = 54 Mbps. 802.11ac = 3.5 Gbps. 802.11ad = 7 Gbps (60 GHz WiGig, the fastest).",
   c:"Chronology and speed rise together: <b>b (11M) &rarr; a/g (54M) &rarr; n (600M) &rarr; ac (3.5G) &rarr; ad (7G) &rarr; ax/Wi-Fi 6</b>. Anchor on the two extremes &mdash; b is the slowest, ad the fastest &mdash; and the middle usually falls into place."},
  {n:12,s:"Computer Organization & Architecture",q:"In a vectored interrupt",
   o:["The branch address is assigned to a fixed memory location","The interrupting source supplies the branch information to the processor","The branch address comes from a processor register","None of the above"],a:1,
   e:"&ldquo;Vectored&rdquo; means the device supplies a vector identifying its own handler. Option A describes a <i>non-vectored</i> interrupt, where control always jumps to one fixed address.",
   c:"<b>Vectored:</b> device supplies the address or an index into the interrupt vector table &mdash; fast, scales to many devices. <b>Non-vectored:</b> fixed handler address, which must then poll to find who interrupted. Also know maskable vs non-maskable, and that a <b>trap</b> is software-generated while an interrupt is hardware-generated.",
   w:"COA had 5 questions in 2023 and <b>zero in 2025</b>. Treat it as low-priority but not abandoned &mdash; see the Trends tab."},
  {n:13,s:"Computer Organization & Architecture",q:"For a finite number of instructions, which processor architecture gives fastest execution?",
   o:["ANSA","Super-scalar","ISA","All of the above"],a:1,
   e:"A super-scalar processor issues multiple instructions per clock cycle through duplicated functional units, so it beats purely scalar designs. ISA is a specification, not a performance architecture; ANSA is a networking term.",
   c:"<b>Pipelining</b> overlaps stages of different instructions (ideal speedup = number of stages). <b>Super-scalar</b> goes further by issuing several instructions in the <i>same</i> cycle, giving IPC &gt; 1. <b>VLIW</b> pushes the scheduling into the compiler instead of hardware."},
  {n:14,s:"Computer Organization & Architecture",q:"An instruction pipeline can be implemented by means of",o:["LIFO buffer","FIFO buffer","Stack","None of the above"],a:1,
   e:"Instructions must leave the pipeline in the order they entered, which is first-in-first-out.",
   c:"Pipeline hazards and their fixes: <b>structural</b> (resource conflict &rarr; duplicate the unit), <b>data</b> (RAW/WAR/WAW &rarr; forwarding or stalls), <b>control</b> (branches &rarr; prediction, delayed branch). Speedup = nk / (k + n &minus; 1) for n instructions through k stages, approaching k for large n."},
  {n:15,s:"Computer Organization & Architecture",q:"The operation executed on data stored in registers is called",o:["Macro-operation","Micro-operation","Bit-operation","Byte-operation"],a:1,
   e:"A micro-operation is an elementary register-level operation performed in one clock pulse.",
   c:"Four classes: <b>register transfer</b> (move between registers), <b>arithmetic</b>, <b>logic</b>, <b>shift</b>. A machine instruction decomposes into a sequence of micro-operations, which is what the control unit sequences &mdash; either hardwired or microprogrammed."},
  {n:16,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Which method is used to detect double errors?",o:["Odd Parity","Even Parity","Checksum (CRC)","Checksum (XOR)"],a:2,
   e:"A single parity bit detects only an odd number of errors &mdash; two errors cancel and go unseen. CRC detects all double-bit errors (and all burst errors shorter than the generator).",
   c:"<b>CRC guarantees:</b> all single-bit errors, all double-bit errors (with a suitable generator), all odd numbers of errors if the generator has factor (x+1), and all bursts shorter than the generator's degree. Computed as polynomial division mod 2; the remainder is the checksum. Compare 2025 Q75 on Hamming distance for the correction side."},
  {n:17,s:"Algorithms",q:"f&#8321;(n)=2&#8319;, f&#8322;(n)=n<sup>3/2</sup>, f&#8323;(n)=n log n, f&#8324;(n)=n<sup>log n</sup>. Increasing order of complexity?",
   o:["f&#8322;, f&#8323;, f&#8324;, f&#8321;","f&#8321;, f&#8322;, f&#8323;, f&#8324;","f&#8323;, f&#8322;, f&#8324;, f&#8321;","f&#8323;, f&#8321;, f&#8322;, f&#8324;"],a:2,
   e:"n log n &lt; n<sup>1.5</sup> &lt; n<sup>log n</sup> &lt; 2&#8319;. The subtle pair is n<sup>1.5</sup> vs n<sup>log n</sup>: the exponent log n eventually exceeds the constant 1.5, so n<sup>log n</sup> wins for large n.",
   c:"<b>Standard growth ladder:</b> 1 &lt; log n &lt; &radic;n &lt; n &lt; n log n &lt; n&sup2; &lt; n&sup3; &lt; n<sup>log n</sup> &lt; 2&#8319; &lt; n! &lt; n&#8319;. For an n<sup>f(n)</sup> form, take logs: f(n)&middot;log n. Comparing n<sup>1.5</sup> and n<sup>log n</sup> reduces to comparing 1.5 against log n &mdash; a constant against something unbounded."},
  {n:18,s:"Algorithms",q:"Maximum number of comparisons to sort 6 items using radix sort, each a 3-digit decimal number?",o:["120","180","210","360"],a:1,
   e:"3 passes (one per digit) &times; 10 buckets &times; 6 items = <b>180</b>.",
   c:"Radix sort is <b>O(d &times; (n + b))</b> for d digits, n items, base b. It is <i>not</i> comparison-based, so it escapes the &Omega;(n log n) lower bound. It must be <b>stable</b> at each pass (counting sort is the usual inner sort), otherwise earlier digit ordering is destroyed. LSD processes least-significant digit first."},
  {n:19,s:"Data Structures",f:1,q:"What is the inorder predecessor of 15 in the BST shown?",o:["18","7","9","13"],a:3,
   e:"Tree is a diagram. In a BST the inorder predecessor is the <b>largest value smaller than the node</b>.",
   c:"<b>Predecessor rule:</b> if the node has a left subtree, it is the <i>rightmost</i> node of that subtree. Otherwise it is the nearest ancestor from whose <i>right</i> subtree the node descends. Successor is the exact mirror: leftmost of the right subtree, else nearest ancestor from whose left subtree it descends. Inorder of a BST is sorted, so predecessor = previous element in sorted order."},
  {n:20,s:"Databases",q:"In SQL, <span class='mono'>select * from R, S</span> is equivalent to",
   o:["R natural join S","R cross join S","R outer join S","R inner join S"],a:1,
   e:"A comma in the FROM clause with no WHERE condition is a Cartesian product &mdash; every row of R paired with every row of S.",
   c:"<b>Cross join</b> = Cartesian product, |R|&times;|S| rows. Adding a WHERE condition turns it into an inner join (theta join). <b>Natural join</b> matches automatically on same-named columns and removes duplicates of them. Outer joins preserve unmatched rows from the left, right or both sides with NULLs."},
  {n:21,s:"Databases",q:"Which SQL deletes all tuples in <i>teacher</i> for teachers whose department is in a building named CSE?",
   o:["DELETE FROM teacher WHERE dept_name IN 'CSE'","DELETE FROM department WHERE building='CSE'","DELETE FROM teacher WHERE dept_name IN (SELECT dept_name FROM department WHERE building='CSE')","None of the above"],a:2,
   e:"The building name lives in <i>department</i>, not <i>teacher</i>, so you need a subquery to translate building &rarr; dept_name. Option A compares a department name against a building name; option B deletes from the wrong table entirely.",
   c:"<b>Check which table holds each attribute mentioned in the condition.</b> If the filter attribute is in a different table from the target, a subquery or join is mandatory. <span class='mono'>IN (subquery)</span> is the standard pattern; <span class='mono'>EXISTS</span> is the correlated alternative."},
  {n:22,s:"Databases",q:"Fifth Normal Form is concerned with",o:["Join dependency","Domain-key","Multivalued dependency","Functional dependency"],a:0,
   e:"5NF (project&ndash;join normal form) eliminates <b>join dependencies</b> &mdash; cases where a relation can be losslessly decomposed into three or more relations but not into two.",
   c:"<b>The normal form ladder by dependency type:</b> 1NF atomic values &rarr; 2NF no partial FD &rarr; 3NF no transitive FD &rarr; BCNF every determinant is a superkey &rarr; <b>4NF no multivalued dependency</b> &rarr; <b>5NF no join dependency</b> &rarr; 6NF/DKNF. Note 4NF is the MVD one and 5NF the JD one &mdash; that is the pair most often swapped."},
  {n:23,s:"Digital Logic & Design",q:"The logic circuit obtained when both inputs of NOR and NAND gates are connected together is",o:["AND","XOR","OR","NOT"],a:3,
   e:"NAND(A,A) = &not;(A&middot;A) = &not;A, and NOR(A,A) = &not;(A+A) = &not;A. Both become inverters.",
   c:"This is why <b>NAND and NOR are functionally complete</b> &mdash; tying inputs gives NOT, and from NOT plus the gate itself you can build AND, OR and therefore everything. Any Boolean function can be implemented with NAND alone or NOR alone, which is why real fabrication favours them."},
  {n:24,s:"Engineering Mathematics",q:"P = [[3,2,1,4],[4,0,3,1],[6,4,2,8],[2,5,1,3]]. Which is true about det|P|?",
   o:["Indeterminate","Negative","det|P| = 0","None of the above"],a:2,
   e:"Row 3 = 2 &times; Row 1 (6,4,2,8 = 2&times;(3,2,1,4)). A row that is a scalar multiple of another makes the matrix singular, so the determinant is 0 without any expansion.",
   c:"<b>Scan for dependence before computing any 4&times;4 determinant.</b> Check: equal rows/columns, one a multiple of another, a zero row, or a row that is a sum of others. For a 4&times;4, cofactor expansion is 24 multiplications &mdash; spotting dependence takes five seconds.",
   w:"Exactly the same trick as 2025 Q27, which used the sin&sup2;+cos&sup2; identity to hide the dependence. <b>Determinant questions in this exam are nearly always dependence-spotting, not computation.</b>"},
  {n:25,s:"Engineering Mathematics",q:"A bag has 6 blue, 5 white and 4 yellow balls. One ball is drawn. Probability of <b>not</b> getting a white ball?",o:["2/3","1/3","1/5","None of the above"],a:0,
   e:"Total 15, non-white = 6+4 = 10, so 10/15 = <b>2/3</b>. Equivalently 1 &minus; 5/15.",
   c:"<b>For &ldquo;not&rdquo; events always use the complement: P(not A) = 1 &minus; P(A).</b> It is faster and avoids missing a category. Extend to &ldquo;at least one&rdquo; problems: P(at least one) = 1 &minus; P(none), which is almost always the intended route."},
  {n:26,s:"Discrete Mathematics",q:"From a character string of length m, the number of substrings of all lengths that can be formed is",o:["m&sup2;","m","m(m+1)/2","m log m"],a:2,
   e:"A substring is fixed by choosing a start and end position: substrings of length 1 number m, length 2 number m&minus;1, and so on down to 1. The sum is m(m+1)/2.",
   c:"<b>Substring (contiguous) vs subsequence (not necessarily contiguous).</b> Substrings: m(m+1)/2 non-empty, or +1 counting the empty one. <b>Subsequences: 2<sup>m</sup></b>. That factor is the usual trap &mdash; read which word the question uses."},
  {n:27,s:"Digital Logic & Design",q:"A truth table needs how many rows for n variables?",o:["2&times;n","n","2&#8319;","None of the above"],a:2,
   e:"Each variable is independently 0 or 1, so 2<sup>n</sup> combinations.",
   c:"Same base-and-exponent logic as 2025 Q62: <b>base = choices per position, exponent = number of positions</b>. Related: the number of distinct Boolean <i>functions</i> of n variables is 2<sup>2&#8319;</sup>, since each of the 2&#8319; rows can independently output 0 or 1."},
  {n:28,s:"Operating System",q:"In segmentation, which component assigns segment numbers for code and data segments of a program?",o:["Compiler","Assembler","Loader","Linker"],a:2,
   e:"The loader assigns segment numbers at load time, when the program is placed in memory.",
   c:"Division of labour: <b>compiler</b> source &rarr; assembly; <b>assembler</b> assembly &rarr; object code; <b>linker</b> combines object modules and resolves external references; <b>loader</b> brings the image into memory and performs final address binding. <b>Address binding can happen at compile time (absolute), load time (relocatable), or execution time (needs MMU)</b> &mdash; that three-way split is the commonly tested point."},
  {n:29,s:"Theory of Computation",f:1,q:"For the NFA shown, the number of states in the MINIMAL DFA is",o:["2","4","6","8"],a:1,
   e:"Automaton is a diagram.",
   c:"Two steps: <b>subset construction</b> NFA &rarr; DFA (an n-state NFA can need up to 2&#8319; DFA states), then <b>table-filling minimisation</b>. Remember to delete unreachable states first. Myhill&ndash;Nerode guarantees the minimal DFA is unique.",
   w:"Minimal-DFA counting appeared in 2023 (here) and 2025 (Q18). It is a fixture of the TOC section."},
  {n:30,s:"Theory of Computation",q:"Grammar G: S &rarr; aSb | T, &nbsp; T &rarr; bT | b. The language generated by G is:",
   o:["{a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : 0 &le; m &lt; n}","{a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : 0 &lt; m &lt; n}","{a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : 0 &lt; n &le; m}","{a<sup>n</sup>b<sup>n</sup> : 0 &le; n}"],a:0,
   e:"T generates b<sup>k</sup> with k &ge; 1. Applying S&rarr;aSb m times then S&rarr;T gives a<sup>m</sup>b<sup>k+m</sup>, so n &gt; m with m allowed to be 0.",
   c:"Decide with the shortest strings. The string <b>b</b> (m = 0) is derivable and separates option A from option B.",
   w:"<b>This is the identical question to 2025 Q17 &mdash; same grammar, same options, same answer, two years apart.</b> It is the clearest evidence in this dataset that ISRO reuses questions verbatim, and the strongest single argument for working old papers."},
  {n:31,s:"Theory of Computation",q:"Which statement is NOT true?",
   o:["If a language is recursive its complement is recursive","If a language is recursive its complement is recursively enumerable","If a language and its complement are both RE, it is recursive","If a language is RE its complement is also RE"],a:3,
   e:"Recursive languages are closed under complement; RE languages are <b>not</b>. The halting problem is RE but its complement is not.",
   c:"<b>The key theorem:</b> L is recursive &hArr; both L and its complement are RE. Recursive = decidable (always halts). RE = semi-decidable (halts on yes, may loop on no). Closure: recursive languages are closed under union, intersection and <b>complement</b>; RE under union and intersection but <b>not complement</b>."},
  {n:32,s:"Discrete Mathematics",q:"Which is not a connective of First Order Logic in AI?",o:["&and;","&or;","&lt;&gt;","&not;"],a:2,
   e:"The standard connectives are &not;, &and;, &or;, &rarr; and &harr;. The symbol &lt;&gt; is not among them.",
   c:"FOL adds <b>quantifiers</b> &forall; and &exist; plus predicates and functions on top of propositional logic. Note &forall;x P(x) &equiv; &not;&exist;x &not;P(x) and &exist;x P(x) &equiv; &not;&forall;x &not;P(x) &mdash; the quantifier De Morgan pair, which is what most FOL questions actually test."},
  {n:33,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"Which scenario may <b>not</b> be a good fit for HDFS?",
   o:["Scenarios requiring multiple simultaneous writes to the same file","Applications requiring low latency data access","Applications requiring high latency data access","None of the above"],a:0,
   e:"HDFS follows a <b>write-once, read-many</b> model with a single writer per file, so concurrent writes to one file are not supported.",
   c:"HDFS is a poor fit for: concurrent writers, low-latency random access (use HBase), many small files (NameNode memory), and arbitrary file modification. It is a good fit for large streaming reads and batch throughput.",
   w:"HDFS was asked in <b>both</b> 2023 and 2025. Near-certain to appear again."},
  {n:34,s:"Object Oriented Programming & Java",q:"A standard Java API for monitoring and managing applications is",o:["JVM","JVN","JMX","JMY"],a:2,
   e:"JMX &mdash; Java Management Extensions &mdash; exposes manageable resources as MBeans for monitoring and control.",
   c:"The Java acronym family worth separating: <b>JVM</b> runtime, <b>JRE</b> runtime + libraries, <b>JDK</b> + tools, <b>JDBC</b> database connectivity, <b>JNDI</b> naming and directory, <b>JMS</b> messaging, <b>JMX</b> management. In a four-option question with two invented lookalikes (JVN, JMY), the real acronyms are the only candidates."},
  {n:35,s:"Databases",q:"Which type of data storage system is Cassandra?",o:["Distributed","Centralized","Parallel","Dumb"],a:0,
   e:"Cassandra is a distributed, masterless column-family NoSQL store, designed so every node is equal and there is no single point of failure.",
   c:"Cassandra is the canonical <b>AP</b> system under CAP &mdash; it favours availability and partition tolerance with eventual consistency, using a ring topology and consistent hashing. Contrast MongoDB and HBase, which are CP. Its write-optimised LSM design suits time-series and high-ingest workloads."},
  {n:36,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"Cloud infrastructure solely for a single organisation within its premises is",o:["public cloud","private cloud","common cloud","hybrid cloud"],a:1,
   e:"Single organisation, on its own premises &mdash; a private cloud.",
   c:"<b>Deployment models:</b> public (shared, provider-owned), private (single organisation), community (shared by organisations with common concerns), hybrid (two or more linked, enabling cloud bursting). Keep these separate from the <b>service models</b> IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, which describe <i>what you manage</i> rather than <i>who owns it</i>."},
  {n:37,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"Temporarily renting capacity to handle spikes in load is called",o:["Cloud sharing","Cloud computing","Cloud bursting","Cloud loading"],a:2,
   e:"Cloud bursting: an application runs in a private cloud and &ldquo;bursts&rdquo; into a public cloud when demand spikes.",
   c:"Cloud bursting is the classic justification for a <b>hybrid</b> deployment &mdash; keep steady-state load on owned infrastructure and rent only the peak. It is an application of <b>elasticity</b> (see 2025 Q82), which is the automatic bidirectional scaling that makes it possible."},
  {n:38,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"In which virtualization technique does the hypervisor modify the guest OS?",o:["Full virtualization","Para virtualization","Both","None"],a:1,
   e:"Paravirtualisation requires the guest OS to be modified to make hypercalls instead of privileged instructions. Full virtualisation leaves the guest unmodified and emulates the hardware.",
   c:"<b>Full:</b> unmodified guest, binary translation or hardware assist (Intel VT-x, AMD-V), better compatibility. <b>Para:</b> modified guest, lower overhead, classic Xen. <b>Containers</b> are OS-level virtualisation sharing one kernel &mdash; not a hypervisor at all.",
   w:"Virtualisation appeared in 2023 (here) and 2025 (Q43 on hypervisor types). Expect it again."},
  {n:39,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Match: (i) Encryption (ii) MAC Cloning (iii) ARP Spoofing (iv) Finger printing &mdash; with (P) manipulating host identification via NIC identifier, (Q) determining OS version and utilities, (R) protecting confidentiality, (S) poisoning IP-to-MAC mapping.",
   o:["(i)R (ii)P (iii)S (iv)Q","(i)P (ii)R (iii)S (iv)Q","(i)R (ii)S (iii)P (iv)Q","(i)R (ii)Q (iii)S (iv)P"],a:0,
   e:"Encryption &rarr; confidentiality (R). MAC cloning &rarr; NIC identifier manipulation (P). ARP spoofing &rarr; poisoning IP-to-MAC mapping (S). Fingerprinting &rarr; OS identification (Q).",
   c:"<b>Match on the distinguishing noun:</b> NIC identifier can only be MAC cloning; IP-to-MAC mapping can only be ARP. ARP spoofing enables man-in-the-middle on a LAN because ARP has no authentication; the defence is dynamic ARP inspection or static entries."},
  {n:40,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Asymmetric encryption is not suitable for",o:["Confidentiality","Authentication","Key Exchange","Prevention of Denial of Service"],a:3,
   e:"Asymmetric cryptography provides confidentiality, authentication and key exchange. <b>DoS is an availability attack</b> &mdash; no encryption scheme prevents an attacker from flooding you with traffic. In fact asymmetric operations are expensive, so they can <i>worsen</i> DoS exposure.",
   c:"Map each mechanism to the CIA property it serves. <b>Encryption &rarr; confidentiality. Hash/MAC &rarr; integrity. Signature &rarr; integrity + authentication + non-repudiation. Nothing cryptographic &rarr; availability</b>, which needs rate limiting, filtering and capacity instead."},
  {n:41,s:"Software Engineering",q:"Regression testing focuses on validating",o:["module structure","module interfaces","software changes","None of the above"],a:2,
   e:"Regression testing re-runs existing tests to confirm that a change has not broken previously working behaviour.",
   c:"It answers &ldquo;did this change break anything?&rdquo; rather than &ldquo;does the new feature work?&rdquo;. Because it runs on every change it is the prime candidate for automation. Selection strategies: retest-all, selective regression, and prioritisation by risk."},
  {n:42,s:"Software Engineering",q:"A HIPO chart is also known as",o:["UML diagram","Structure chart","DFD","None of the above"],a:1,
   e:"HIPO (Hierarchy plus Input-Process-Output) depicts the module hierarchy of a system, which is what a structure chart shows.",
   c:"Keep the design notations apart: <b>structure chart / HIPO</b> shows module hierarchy and call relationships; <b>DFD</b> shows data flow between processes and stores (no control flow, no timing); <b>ER diagram</b> shows data relationships; <b>UML</b> is the object-oriented family. A DFD is explicitly <i>not</i> a flowchart."},
  {n:43,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which option replaces the question mark?",o:["figure A","figure B","figure C","figure D"],a:3,
   e:"Figure-based.",
   c:"Same discipline as 2025 Q1 and Q8 &mdash; tabulate count, position, rotation and superposition independently, derive the rule from the complete rows, then check the options."},
  {n:44,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which option replaces the question mark?",o:["A","B","C","D"],a:3,e:"Figure-based.",c:"See the method notes on the other pattern questions."},
  {n:45,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which option replaces the question mark?",o:["A","B","C","D"],a:1,e:"Figure-based.",c:"See the method notes on the other pattern questions."},
  {n:46,s:"General Aptitude",q:"You usually work Monday&ndash;Friday. The new schedule gives you a Saturday shift, but it is your son's birthday with a party planned. Most appropriate response?",
   o:["Speak with your manager, explain, and ask if someone else can cover or offer to find cover","Ask coworkers to cover because you have a party","Pretend you did not see the schedule and do not show up","Say nothing and skip it"],a:0,
   e:"The correct answer goes to the <b>person with authority over the schedule</b> first, explains honestly, and offers a constructive solution. Option B skips the manager; C and D are avoidance.",
   c:"<b>Situational judgement questions follow a fixed hierarchy:</b> (1) communicate with the right person, (2) be honest, (3) offer a solution rather than only a problem, (4) never avoid or deceive. The option combining notification <i>and</i> a proposed fix is almost always correct.",
   w:"ISRO included several situational-judgement items in 2023 General Aptitude and none in 2025. They cost no preparation &mdash; just apply the hierarchy above."},
  {n:49,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which option replaces the question mark?",o:["1","2","3","4"],a:0,e:"Figure-based.",c:"See the pattern-grid method."},
  {n:50,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which one will replace the question mark?",o:["45","8","38","48"],a:2,
   e:"Figure-based number puzzle.",
   c:"Test templates in order: outer numbers combined to give the centre; diagonal sums differenced; product of a pair divided by another. Validate on every complete figure before applying."},
  {n:51,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"Which option replaces the question mark?",o:["A","B","C","D"],a:2,e:"Figure-based.",c:"See the pattern-grid method."},
  {n:52,s:"General Aptitude",q:"Fact 1: Pictures can tell a story. Fact 2: All storybooks have pictures. Fact 3: Some storybooks have words.<br>Which must also be a fact? I: Pictures tell a story better than words. II: Stories in storybooks are very simple. III: Some storybooks have both words and pictures.",
   o:["I only","II only","III only","None of the statements is a known fact"],a:2,
   e:"Fact 2 says <b>all</b> storybooks have pictures; Fact 3 says <b>some</b> have words. Those some-with-words necessarily also have pictures, so III is forced. I makes a comparison nothing supports; II introduces &ldquo;simple&rdquo;, a term absent from the facts.",
   c:"<b>Combine a universal with a particular to get a valid particular conclusion.</b> Reject any statement containing a comparative (&ldquo;better&rdquo;) or a new attribute (&ldquo;simple&rdquo;) not present in the premises &mdash; that single filter kills most distractors in these questions."},
  {n:55,s:"General Aptitude",f:1,q:"The graph shows variation in earnings over a week. The difference in earnings was largest between",
   o:["Sunday-Monday","Tuesday-Wednesday","Thursday-Friday","None of these"],a:0,
   e:"Graph is an image.",
   c:"For data-interpretation questions, read what is asked precisely: <b>difference</b> (absolute gap) is not <b>percentage change</b> (gap relative to the starting value). The largest absolute change and the largest percentage change are frequently different pairs, and both appear as options."},
  {n:56,s:"General Aptitude",q:"Production of D type toys: 1983 = 105, 1985 = 125. Approximate percentage increase?",o:["10","20","19","76"],a:2,
   e:"(125 &minus; 105)/105 = 20/105 = <b>19.05%</b>.",
   c:"<b>Percentage change = (new &minus; old) / old &times; 100</b> &mdash; always divide by the <i>original</i> value. Dividing by the new value instead gives 16%, and dividing by neither gives 20, which is option B. That division is the entire question."},
  {n:57,s:"General Aptitude",q:"What is the maximum area of a rectangle with perimeter 620 mm?",o:["24,025 mm&sup2;","22,725 mm&sup2;","24,000 mm&sup2;","24,075 mm&sup2;"],a:0,
   e:"Area is maximised when the rectangle is a <b>square</b>: side = 620/4 = 155, area = 155&sup2; = <b>24,025</b>.",
   c:"<b>For a fixed perimeter the square maximises area; for a fixed area the square minimises perimeter.</b> The general principle (isoperimetric) extends to the circle among all plane figures. Spotting that the optimum is the symmetric case turns an optimisation into one multiplication."},
  {n:61,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Which of the following is true?",
   o:["Both Parity and CRC are error correcting codes","Both Parity and LDPC are error correcting codes","Both Reed-Solomon and LDPC are error correcting codes","Both CRC and LDPC are error correcting codes"],a:2,
   e:"Parity and CRC are error <b>detecting</b> only. Reed&ndash;Solomon and LDPC are genuine error <b>correcting</b> codes.",
   c:"<b>Detection only:</b> parity, checksum, CRC. <b>Correction:</b> Hamming, Reed&ndash;Solomon, LDPC, Turbo, convolutional. Correction needs enough redundancy for d<sub>min</sub> &ge; 2t+1 (see 2025 Q75). Reed&ndash;Solomon is used in CDs, DVDs and deep-space links &mdash; which is why it is relevant to ISRO specifically.",
   w:"Coding theory appeared in both years. Given the organisation, <b>Reed&ndash;Solomon and burst-error correction are worth knowing by name.</b>"},
  {n:62,s:"Data Structures",q:"In Reverse Polish notation, A*B + C*D is written as",o:["AB*CD*+","A*BCD*+","AB*CD+*","A*B*CD+"],a:0,
   e:"Convert each product first: A*B &rarr; AB*, C*D &rarr; CD*. Then the addition of the two results appends +: <b>AB*CD*+</b>.",
   c:"<b>Postfix conversion:</b> fully parenthesise by precedence, then move each operator just after its two operands. To <i>evaluate</i> postfix, push operands and pop two on each operator. Prefix (Polish) is the mirror: operator before operands. An expression tree gives all three by traversal &mdash; inorder = infix, preorder = prefix, postorder = postfix."},
  {n:63,s:"Programming & C Language",q:"Pointer p to an array of integers (2 bytes each) is initialised to 200. What is p+3?",o:["206","203","212","204"],a:0,
   e:"Pointer arithmetic scales by the element size: 200 + 3&times;2 = <b>206</b>.",
   c:"<b>p + n advances by n &times; sizeof(type) bytes</b>, not n bytes &mdash; the compiler scales automatically. Option B (203) is the un-scaled answer and is the whole point of the question. Also: the difference of two pointers gives the number of <i>elements</i> between them, not bytes."},
  {n:64,s:"Data Structures",q:"In a priority queue, insertion and deletion can be done at",o:["Front","Back","Middle","Any position"],a:3,
   e:"Position in a priority queue is determined by <b>priority</b>, not by arrival order, so an element may enter or leave anywhere in the logical ordering.",
   c:"Usually implemented as a <b>binary heap</b>: insert and extract-min both O(log n), peek O(1), build-heap O(n). Contrast a plain queue (FIFO, both ends fixed), a stack (LIFO, one end), and a deque (both ends). Heaps underpin Dijkstra, Prim and heap sort."},
  {n:65,s:"Algorithms",q:"Worst case time complexity of heap sort for n elements",o:["O(n log n)","O(log n)","O(n&sup2;)","O(n)"],a:0,
   e:"n extract-max operations at O(log n) each, after an O(n) build-heap.",
   c:"<b>Heap sort is the only common sort that is O(n log n) in the worst case <i>and</i> O(1) space.</b> Merge sort matches the time but needs O(n) space; quicksort matches on average but degrades to O(n&sup2;). Heap sort is not stable. Build-heap is O(n), not O(n log n) &mdash; a frequently tested subtlety."},
  {n:66,s:"Databases",q:"Table T(X,Y) starts with (1,1). 128 inserts follow, each adding (MX+1, 2&times;MY+1) where MX, MY are the current maxima. What does <span class='mono'>SELECT Y FROM T WHERE X = 7</span> return?",
   o:["127","15","129","257"],a:0,
   e:"X increments by 1 each time: 1, 2, 3, &hellip; so X = 7 is the 7th row. Y follows Y&#8342; = 2Y&#8342;&#8331;&#8321;+1 from Y&#8321;=1: 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, 63, <b>127</b> &mdash; that is 2<sup>k</sup> &minus; 1.",
   c:"<b>Recognise the recurrence rather than iterating 128 times.</b> a&#8342; = 2a&#8342;&#8331;&#8321; + 1 with a&#8321; = 1 solves to 2<sup>k</sup> &minus; 1 (add 1 to both sides to see the pure doubling). The 128 inserts are a red herring &mdash; only the 7th row is queried."},
  {n:67,s:"Databases",q:"Among the ACID properties, 'Durability' requires that changes made by a successful transaction persist",
   o:["Except in case of an OS crash","Except in case of a disk failure","(see paper)","(see paper)"],a:3,
   e:"Durability means committed changes survive <b>any</b> subsequent failure &mdash; power loss, OS crash, or system failure. The options carving out exceptions are what make them wrong.",
   c:"<b>A</b>tomicity: all or nothing. <b>C</b>onsistency: constraints preserved. <b>I</b>solation: concurrent transactions do not interfere. <b>D</b>urability: committed means permanent, enforced by the write-ahead log and forced log writes at commit. Durability is about committed data only &mdash; an uncommitted transaction has no such guarantee."},
  {n:72,s:"Digital Logic & Design",q:"A logic circuit giving LOW output when both inputs are HIGH or both are LOW is",o:["AND","NAND","XNOR","XOR"],a:3,
   e:"Output LOW when inputs are equal means output HIGH when they differ &mdash; that is <b>XOR</b>.",
   c:"<b>XOR = 1 when inputs differ; XNOR = 1 when inputs are equal</b> (so XNOR is the equality/comparator gate). Useful identities: A&oplus;0 = A, A&oplus;1 = &Acirc;&not;A, A&oplus;A = 0, and XOR is associative and commutative &mdash; which is why it builds parity trees and adders."},
  {n:73,s:"Discrete Mathematics",q:"Which are true? (i) &Sigma;deg = 2e (ii) Eccentricity can never equal the radius (iii) Girth is the shortest cycle (iv) A graph with equal degree for all vertices is a multigraph",
   o:["(i),(ii),(iii)","(ii),(iii),(iv)","(i),(iii),(iv)","None of the above"],a:3,
   e:"(i) and (iii) are true, but (ii) is false &mdash; the <b>radius is defined as the minimum eccentricity</b>, so some vertex (the centre) always attains it. (iv) is false &mdash; equal degrees means <b>regular</b>, which has nothing to do with being a multigraph. Since no option lists exactly {(i),(iii)}, the answer is None.",
   c:"<b>Eccentricity</b> of v = greatest distance from v to any vertex. <b>Radius</b> = min eccentricity, <b>diameter</b> = max eccentricity, <b>centre</b> = the vertices achieving the radius. <b>Girth</b> = length of the shortest cycle (infinite for a forest). <b>Regular</b> = all degrees equal; <b>multigraph</b> = parallel edges allowed.",
   w:"When a &ldquo;which are true&rdquo; option set contains no combination matching your analysis, <b>None of the above</b> is the answer &mdash; do not force a fit."},
  {n:74,s:"Discrete Mathematics",q:"An undirected planar graph has 5 faces and 9 vertices. The number of edges is",o:["14","16","12","None of the above"],a:2,
   e:"Euler: V &minus; E + F = 2 &rArr; 9 &minus; E + 5 = 2 &rArr; E = <b>12</b>.",
   c:"<b>V &minus; E + F = 2</b> for a connected planar graph; for C components it is V &minus; E + F = 1 + C. Sanity-check against E &le; 3V &minus; 6 (here 12 &le; 21 &check;).",
   w:"Euler's formula was asked in 2023 (here, straightforwardly) and 2025 (Q50, dressed up as shopping malls and roads). <b>It is a near-certain repeat.</b>"},
  {n:75,s:"Algorithms",q:"Complexity of multiplying matrices of order m&times;n and n&times;p is",o:["O(m&times;p)","O(m&times;n&sup2;&times;p)","O(m&times;n&times;p&sup2;)","O(m&times;n&times;p)"],a:3,
   e:"The result has m&times;p entries and each requires a dot product of length n, giving <b>O(mnp)</b>.",
   c:"For square matrices this is the familiar O(n&sup3;). <b>Strassen</b> reduces it to O(n<sup>2.807</sup>) by trading one multiplication for several additions. Do not confuse this with <b>matrix chain multiplication</b>, which is the dynamic-programming problem of choosing the best parenthesisation, at O(n&sup3;) in the number of matrices."},
  {n:78,s:"Computer Organization & Architecture",q:"Which is false about interrupts?",
   o:["Interrupts can be triggered by hardware or software","Hardware interrupts may be triggered by a signal on the system bus","Software interrupts may be triggered by system calls","Trap is a hardware generated interrupt"],a:3,
   e:"A <b>trap is software-generated</b> &mdash; raised by the executing program, either deliberately (a system call) or by an error such as divide-by-zero. That makes option D the false statement.",
   c:"<b>Interrupt = asynchronous, external, hardware. Trap/exception = synchronous, internal, software.</b> A trap is reproducible because it occurs at a fixed point in the instruction stream; an interrupt can arrive at any time. System calls are implemented as deliberate traps, which is how user code transfers control to the kernel."},
  {n:79,s:"Operating System",q:"Match process schedulers: (i) Long Term (ii) Medium Term (iii) Short Term &mdash; with (P) executes much faster to reduce wastage of CPU time, (Q) controls degree of multiprogramming, (R) associated with swapping.",
   o:["(i)R (ii)P (iii)Q","(i)Q (ii)R (iii)P","(i)P (ii)Q (iii)R","(i)Q (ii)P (iii)R"],a:1,
   e:"Long term admits jobs, so it <b>controls the degree of multiprogramming</b> (Q). Medium term <b>swaps</b> processes in and out (R). Short term runs most frequently so it must be <b>fast</b> (P).",
   c:"<b>Frequency is the organising idea:</b> long term runs rarely (seconds to minutes) and decides <i>which jobs enter</i>; medium term runs occasionally and decides <i>who stays in memory</i>; short term runs constantly (milliseconds) and decides <i>who gets the CPU next</i> &mdash; so it must be cheap to execute."},
  {n:84,s:"Theory of Computation",q:"Grammar G: S &rarr; aaSb | T, &nbsp; T &rarr; Tb | a. Which statement is true about L(G)?",
   o:["aabbaabb &isin; L(G) but aabb &notin;","aaaaabbb &isin; L(G) but aaaabb &notin;","aaaabb &isin; L(G) but aabbaabb &notin;","aaabb &isin; L(G) but aaaaabbb &notin;"],a:1,
   e:"T generates ab<sup>k</sup> (k &ge; 0). S &rarr; aaSb applied n times then T gives <b>a<sup>2n+1</sup> b<sup>k+n</sup></b>. The count of a's is therefore always <b>odd</b>. aaaaabbb = a&#8309;b&sup3; works (n=2, k=1). aaaabb has four a's &mdash; even, so impossible.",
   c:"<b>Derive the closed form, then test a single structural property.</b> Here &ldquo;number of a's is always odd&rdquo; eliminates options without counting b's at all. Rules of the form X &rarr; &alpha;X&beta; | &gamma; always give &alpha;<sup>n</sup>&gamma;&beta;<sup>n</sup> &mdash; note &alpha; = aa contributes two a's per step.",
   w:"Same template as 2025 Q17 and Q77 and 2023 Q30. <b>Three of the four TOC questions across both papers are grammar-closed-form questions.</b> Master the one technique and you cover most of the section."},
  {n:85,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"A hybrid Bayesian network contains",
   o:["Only continuous variables","Only discrete variables","Both discrete and discontinuous variables","Both continuous and discrete variables"],a:3,
   e:"&ldquo;Hybrid&rdquo; means a mixture of continuous and discrete variables. Option C is a near-duplicate with &ldquo;discontinuous&rdquo; substituted &mdash; a deliberate misreading trap.",
   c:"A Bayesian network is a <b>directed acyclic graph</b> where nodes are random variables and edges encode conditional dependence; each node carries P(node | parents). It factorises the joint distribution as a product of these local terms, which is what makes inference tractable.",
   w:"<b>Read near-identical options character by character.</b> &ldquo;Discontinuous&rdquo; vs &ldquo;discrete&rdquo; is the entire difference between a right and wrong answer here."},
  {n:86,s:"AI, Data Science & Cloud",q:"Which clustering technique is used by the K-Means algorithm?",o:["Hierarchical","Partitional","Divisive","Agglomerative"],a:1,
   e:"K-means divides data into k non-overlapping partitions, assigning each point to exactly one cluster. Divisive and agglomerative are the two <i>hierarchical</i> varieties.",
   c:"<b>Partitional:</b> k-means, k-medoids, DBSCAN &mdash; flat clusters, k usually fixed in advance. <b>Hierarchical:</b> agglomerative (bottom-up merging) and divisive (top-down splitting), producing a dendrogram. K-means minimises within-cluster sum of squares, is sensitive to initialisation (hence k-means++) and assumes roughly spherical clusters."},
  {n:89,s:"Computer Networks",q:"Match: (i) Ingress Filtering (ii) Egress Filtering (iii) NAT (iv) Stateful firewall &mdash; with (P) data leakage prevention, (Q) hiding identity of systems, (R) keeping track of TCP/IP connections, (S) malicious traffic prevention.",
   o:["(i)S (ii)P (iii)Q (iv)R","(i)P (ii)R (iii)S (iv)Q","(i)S (ii)R (iii)P (iv)Q","(i)R (ii)Q (iii)S (iv)P"],a:0,
   e:"Ingress filters <b>incoming</b> traffic &rarr; blocks malicious traffic (S). Egress filters <b>outgoing</b> traffic &rarr; prevents data leaving (P). NAT rewrites addresses &rarr; hides internal identity (Q). A stateful firewall tracks connections (R).",
   c:"<b>Ingress = in, egress = out.</b> That single fact resolves the first two. Ingress filtering also blocks spoofed source addresses (BCP 38). Egress filtering is the one most organisations neglect, and it is what catches exfiltration and compromised internal hosts."},
  {n:90,s:"Computer Networks",q:"CAPTCHA is used to provide protection from",o:["Zero day attack","Buffer overflow","Automated scripted attack","Man in the middle attack"],a:2,
   e:"CAPTCHA distinguishes humans from programs, so it defends against bots &mdash; credential stuffing, scripted sign-ups, automated scraping.",
   c:"<b>Match the control to what it actually blocks.</b> Zero-day &rarr; patching and defence in depth. Buffer overflow &rarr; bounds checking, ASLR, DEP, stack canaries. MITM &rarr; TLS and certificate validation. Automated scripts &rarr; CAPTCHA and rate limiting.",
   w:"Attack-to-control matching appeared in 2023 (here, Q39, Q89) and 2025 (Q85, Q88). <b>It is the most repeated question shape in the security material.</b>"},
  {n:91,s:"Software Engineering",q:"MCDC software testing stands for",o:["Multiple Conditions Decision Coverage","Multiple condition... (see paper)","Modified Condition/Decision Coverage","(see paper)"],a:2,
   e:"<b>M</b>odified <b>C</b>ondition / <b>D</b>ecision <b>C</b>overage: every condition in a decision must be shown to independently affect the decision's outcome.",
   c:"The coverage ladder, weakest to strongest: <b>statement &rarr; branch/decision &rarr; condition &rarr; MC/DC &rarr; multiple condition</b>. MC/DC needs only n+1 tests for n conditions, versus 2<sup>n</sup> for full multiple-condition coverage &mdash; which is why it is mandated for safety-critical avionics software under DO-178C. That standards context is exactly why ISRO asks it."}
]});
